# The glossary with every Survival omen and synergy tag added (they live in SurvivalData, so they are
# generated here rather than written twice).
static func glossary_rows() -> Array:
	var rows: Array = []
	for r in GLOSSARY:
		rows.append([r[0], CampaignUI.keys(String(r[1]))])   # 0.23: {action} tokens show the player's binding
	for o in SurvivalData.OMENS:
		var od: Dictionary = SurvivalData.OMENS[o]
		rows.append(["Omen: %s" % od["name"], "%s  An omen arrives with every boss round in Survival and lasts for the rest of the run." % od["desc"]])
	for tg in SurvivalData.TAG_ORDER:
		var td: Dictionary = SurvivalData.TAGS[tg]
		var tiers: Array = []
		for i in SurvivalData.SYN_TIERS.size():
			tiers.append("%d cards: %s" % [SurvivalData.SYN_TIERS[i], td["tiers"][i]])
		rows.append(["Synergy: %s" % td["name"], "Survival synergy tag. Own that many different cards (abilities, passives or items) with the tag:\n" + "\n".join(tiers)])
	return rows


# 0.19: Codex lore for heroes who have no campaign entry (the Codex reads CampaignData.CODEX first).
const HERO_LORE := {
	"corvina": {"text": "The Barrowmoor is where the Vale buries its kings, and the Hallowmeres have kept its mounds for nine generations. Corvina is the last of them. She learned the old covenant from her grandmother: that the dead owe the living one more watch. She does not raise the dead so much as ask them, and so far they have always said yes. The rangers of the Thornmarch give her a wide berth and a warm fire."},
	"ysolde": {"text": "The Brightmeres swore their oath to the moon before there was a Ward to swear to, and Ysolde still keeps it: she hunts only by moonlight, and never alone. Her glaives were forged from a fallen star in the shape of the crescent that fell with it. Those who fight beside her say they feel steadier, braver, sharper; she says it is only the light."},
	# ---- 0.27 briony ----
	"briony": {"text": "The Thornhallows have tended the great hedges of the Verdant Ward since the first briar was planted round the Heartstone, and Briony learned their old work in their old way: by singing to it. Her hymns quicken a tired arm, harden skin to bark and wrap the hurt in living heartwood, and where she plants her staff in anger the hedge answers in thorns. The rangers say the roses on her crown never wilt. She says that is only because she keeps singing."},
	# ---- end 0.27 briony ----
	"malric": {"text": "The barrow-folk of the Grey Fells bury their dead with a candle and a name, and for three hundred years a Sorrowcairn has walked the cairns at night to keep both lit. Malric was the last of them, and he was walking the cairns the night the Hollow broke. In the morning his candles burned green and the dead he had tended stood up when he spoke to them. He does not call it a gift. He calls it a debt: they are his flock still, and he will not leave them to the grey."},   # 0.36
	"wren": {"text": "Wren Halloway kept the field hospital at Riverfork through the three winters of the war, with eleven cots, a saw and a kettle that was never off the fire. She learned that you cannot save everyone, so you save the one who is about to fall, and you do it now. When the Ward marched again she packed the kettle."},
	"vesper": {"text": "The lamp-snuffers walked the Lantern Road at dawn and put out the lights the night had needed. Vesper was the last of them, and when the road went dark for good she found that she was very good at the dark. She works for coin now, and she is quiet about whose lights she puts out."},
	"vasska": {"text": "Under the Deep Sands, where the old river ran before the Rending, the serpent-folk kept a buried temple and slept coiled round its pillars, a century at a time. Vasska woke when the temple fell on him. He dug his way up through the stone with his hands, wearing the pillars on his back, and found the river gone and the Ward at war. The rangers who met him in the dunes say he is still growing. He says that is what serpents do when they have not finished waking: they shed, and come out larger."},   # 0.36
	"cultist": {"text": "He sang in the Pale Choir for eleven years, until the night the Seal closed and the singing stopped. The others walked into the grey and did not come out. He walked out with a mask he cannot remember putting on and a voice under it that is not his, and the voice told him that he would not die until it had finished with him. So far it has kept its word. He fights for the Ward now, because the voice told him to; nobody has asked what it will tell him next."},   # 0.34
	# ---- 0.45 codex ---- lore for the heroes who had none in the Codex (the campaign titles they carry)
	"aurelia": {"text": "The Verdant Ward calls its commander the Lantern, and Aurelia Dawn has carried the title longer than anyone alive. She learned to heal in the infirmary of Heartstone Keep, holding a lamp over the surgeons' hands, and she still fights the same way: from behind the line, keeping everyone in front of her standing. Soldiers who have served under her swear that the light gets brighter when they are hurt."},
	"frostine": {"text": "In the high north the people count winters, not years, and Frostine Vael was born at the end of the longest one in memory. The cold never left her. Snow falls around her in summer; rivers skin over when she crosses them. She came south when the Heartstones began to fail, because she knows better than anyone what a land looks like when its warmth goes out."},
	"grond": {"text": "Dunmar Ironhide stood the gate of Heartstone Keep for thirty years and never once had to close it. He talked to the stone on quiet nights, and it never answered, until the night the rifts opened and it hummed. He wears the gate's old spikes bolted to his armour now. He says a gatekeeper does not need a gate; he only needs something to stand in front of."},
	"hargrim": {"text": "The Deep Smiths forged under the mountains for a thousand years, cutting runes into iron that remembered what it was told. Hargrim Runeforge is the last of them. He carries his anvil on his back and his ancestors' runes on his arms, and every eight seconds, he says, one of them remembers to keep him alive. He has not yet decided whether that is a blessing or a nag."},
	"kael": {"text": "Orrin Stormcaller was a fisherman until a squall took his boat and gave him back its voice. He hears weather now the way other people hear speech, and when he is angry the sky agrees with him. The Ward calls him the Voice of the Squall. He calls himself a fisherman who has not been allowed to go home yet."},
	"kiri": {"text": "Kiri Moonshadow grew up among the paper-lantern streets of the river towns, where she learned that most people never look up. She moves along rooftops and through shadows, a shuriken in each hand, and she always throws two: one for the target and one for whoever was standing behind it. The Ward hired her as a scout. Nobody is quite sure when she started taking orders."},
	"lyra": {"text": "Lyra Starweaver mapped the night sky for the Ward's observatory, star by star, until the night the stars began to move. She has redrawn her charts every evening since. Her spells are borrowed from the constellations she still trusts, and every monster she fells, she says, gives a little of its stolen light back to the sky."},
	"mira": {"text": "Mira Gearwright built her first turret out of a butter churn when she was nine, to keep crows off her father's fields. The Ward's engineers laughed at her until it shot their sergeant's hat off. Now she is the Marshal of Engines, and her machines hold roads while the soldiers rest. She pays for parts out of what the monsters drop. She is never short of parts."},
	"morwen": {"text": "The fen-witches of the Mirelands keep their secrets in pond water, and Morwen Hexweaver kept more of them than most. The Mother of Frogs, the villagers call her, half fond and half afraid. Her hexes make a thing weaker for everyone who hits it, which she considers good manners: why should she do all the work?"},
	"ossian": {"text": "Ossian Deepcaller kept the tide-shrines of the Sunken Coast, where the sea is a god and the god is moody. He learned to speak to it in the old drowned tongue, and sometimes it listens. When the monsters came down the roads he asked the sea for help. It sent him, which he says was its way of saying no."},
	"pip": {"text": "Pip Quickfuse is an alchemist, mostly. He is also a gambler, an inventor of several kinds of bomb, and the reason the Keep's east tower has no roof. Luck follows him like a stray dog. His elixirs work about as often as he says they will, which is to say, more often than anyone expects."},
	"sylra": {"text": "The Thornwood is the oldest forest in the Vale, and its wardens have kept it longer than the Ward has kept the Heartstone. Sylra was raised among its rangers and trusts a bowstring more than a promise. She counts every shot, and the third arrow, the rangers say, is the one the forest guides."},
	"tarek": {"text": "The Dawnsworn take their vows at sunrise and keep them until sunset, every day of their lives. Tarek Sunblade has never broken one. The holy light that burns around him is not a spell, he says, but a habit: the sun is used to him by now. He carries a shield big enough for two because he usually ends up standing in front of someone."},
	"varric": {"text": "Torvan Ashbrand was a smith's apprentice when the forge-fire got into him and would not come out. They call him the Kindled. Everything he strikes catches; everything he touches is warm. He does not mind. He has been cold before, in the years before the fire found him, and he would rather burn."},
	"zephyr": {"text": "The monks of Stormwatch meditate on the highest peak in the Vale, where the wind never st